WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.564
Provenance is information about where media came from, how it was created or captured, and how it reached the analyst.

00:00:08.564 --> 00:00:15.534
It can include the original publisher, upload history, file lineage, timestamps, edits, transfers, and preservation records.

00:00:15.534 --> 00:00:19.318
Start with the claim, not only the file.

00:00:19.318 --> 00:00:28.678
Record what the post says happened, who or what it identifies, the alleged date and location, and the account making the claim.

00:00:28.678 --> 00:00:33.259
A real image paired with a false caption remains misleading.

00:00:33.259 --> 00:00:35.051
Reposts weaken context.

00:00:35.051 --> 00:00:41.623
They may crop watermarks, remove earlier captions, change dates, or combine media from different events.

00:00:41.623 --> 00:00:48.992
Trace backward toward the earliest available appearance and distinguish the first item found from the original source.

00:00:48.992 --> 00:00:50.784
Platform context matters.

00:00:50.784 --> 00:00:57.755
Account history, correction notices, edits, comment chronology, and linked material can clarify how a claim developed.

00:00:57.755 --> 00:01:03.928
Popularity or verification badges do not prove that a specific media claim is accurate.

00:01:03.928 --> 00:01:09.305
Chain of custody records how evidence was obtained, stored, transformed, and reviewed.

00:01:09.305 --> 00:01:16.674
Preserve URLs, access times, available originals, hashes when required by policy, and notes about extraction or conversion.

00:01:16.674 --> 00:01:21.255
Do not overwrite the original with an edited working copy.

00:01:21.255 --> 00:01:25.039
Provenance often provides stronger evidence than appearance alone.

00:01:25.039 --> 00:01:35.594
An authenticated original recording with a documented chain may outweigh vague artifact concerns, while an untraceable viral clip remains uncertain even when it looks convincing.
